You are now on shift. You have read-only access to the fabric devices and the IaC repo. No changes are approved yet.

What evidence do you need to pull next to determine whether the split is causing the flap, and what would you check before proposing any fix?

A change request has been filed against an OT network segment. The segment is a campus-style Layer 2 fabric connecting two control-system zones, Zone A (process control) and Zone B (safety systems), to a pair of redundant distribution switches. The distribution switches are Nokia SR Linux devices. A recent change was made on one distribution switch, `dist-a`, to align its Port-Channel configuration with the other distribution switch, `dist-b`. The change was pushed via an automation pipeline, but the registered network operator did not review the configuration diff before it was applied.

A follow-up change request is now open to "restore the original LACP configuration" on `dist-a`. The request includes a configuration excerpt from `dist-b` that is intended to serve as the reference baseline. The excerpt shows a LAG interface named `lag1` with the following configuration:

```
interface lag1 {
    admin-state enable
    lacp {
        mode active
        interval fast
    }
    member-links {
        link 1 {
            interface ethernet-1/1
        }
        link 2 {
            interface ethernet-1/2
        }
    }
}
```

The change request on `dist-a` proposes to replicate this exact configuration. However, the operator notes that `dist-a` currently has two member links, `ethernet-1/3` and `ethernet-1/4`, configured under `lag1`, and that the original configuration prior to the automation change had `lacp mode passive` and `interval slow`. The proposed change would replace the existing member links and alter the LACP parameters.

The OT environment has a strict change window policy. The current change window closes in 30 minutes. Any misconfiguration could cause a link flap between `dist-a` and the two access switches that connect Zone A and Zone B. The access switches are managed by a separate team and are not part of this change request. A previous, unrelated LAG change in this fabric caused a broadcast storm due to a mismatched LACP mode, so the operations team is sensitive to any change in this area.

The change request is pending your review. You have access to the `dist-a` running configuration, but you have not yet pulled it. You also have access to the automation pipeline's change log, which shows the exact diff that was applied earlier. No monitoring or telemetry data is available for this segment in real time; the only evidence is the configuration state and the change log.

Evaluate the change request. Identify any potential issues with the proposed configuration. Determine whether you can approve the change as-is, whether you need to request additional evidence before proceeding, or whether the change should be rejected or escalated. Justify your decision with specific reasoning based on the evidence provided.

